gcp resource hierarchy
In Google Cloud Platform (GCP), the resource hierarchy refers to the way resources are organized and structured within a project. The hierarchy allows you to manage and control access to resources at different levels, providing a logical and hierarchical structure for your resources. The main components of the GCP resource hierarchy are as follows:
Organization: At the top of the hierarchy is the Organization, which represents your entire GCP environment. It is typically associated with your domain or company. An Organization can contain one or more projects.
Projects: Projects are the next level in the hierarchy and serve as containers for resources. They provide a way to logically group and isolate resources, set permissions, and manage billing. A Project can contain other resources such as Compute Engine instances, storage buckets, databases, and more.
Folder: Folders are optional and provide an additional level of organization within a Project. You can create folders to group related projects or resources together. Folders help in managing permissions and applying policies across multiple projects.
Resources: Resources are the actual services and components you create within a project. These can include virtual machines, databases, cloud storage buckets, networking resources, and more.
The resource hierarchy in GCP follows a tree-like structure, where an Organization can contain multiple Projects, each Project can contain multiple Folders, and each Folder can contain further Folders or Resources.
The resource hierarchy provides benefits such as centralized management, easier administration of permissions and policies, resource isolation, and clear separation of billing and usage.
It's worth noting that the GCP resource hierarchy is flexible and can be customized based on your specific needs. You can adjust the hierarchy as your organization evolves and requirements change.


Cloud IAM (Identity and Access Management) is a service provided by Google Cloud Platform (GCP) that allows you to manage and control access to resources within your GCP environment. It helps you enforce security and access policies by granting or denying permissions to users, groups, and service accounts.
Key concepts in Cloud IAM:
Identity: An identity can represent a Google Account (user), a Google Group, a G Suite or Cloud Identity domain, a service account, or even a Google Workspace or Cloud Identity domain. Identities are associated with a unique email address.
Roles: A role is a collection of permissions that define what actions can be performed on resources. Cloud IAM provides several predefined roles with different levels of access, such as Owner, Editor, and Viewer. You can also create custom roles to fit specific needs.
Permissions: A permission is a specific action that can be performed on a resource, such as reading, writing, creating, or deleting. Permissions are grouped into roles, and roles are assigned to identities.
Policies: A policy is a set of rules that determine who has what level of access to which resources. It defines which roles are assigned to which identities for a particular resource or project.
Resource-level IAM vs. Organization-level IAM: Cloud IAM allows you to set permissions at different levels. Resource-level IAM allows you to control access at the project, folder, or individual resource level. Organization-level IAM sets policies and controls at the organization level, affecting all resources within the organization.
Cloud IAM provides fine-grained access control, allowing you to grant different levels of access to different users or groups. It also enables you to manage access across multiple projects and resources, ensuring a secure and controlled environment.
Using Cloud IAM, you can assign roles and permissions to identities, manage service accounts, audit access logs, and gain a granular understanding of who has access to your resources and what actions they can perform.

